WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
Good morning John, today I'm going to talk about numbers.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.020
Numbers are great because we can't really understand the world

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.240
It's too big

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:14.100
There is, for example no way for me to really understand what a million of something is.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.960
Two, I got two. Two is just one thing twice.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.440
But two million?!

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.380
(Confused Hank noises)

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.280
As an example

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.400
I know how long a second is, and I know how many a million is

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.980
It's just a thousand thousands, and a thousand is just ten hundreds

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:36.360
I can say those words, and so then I should be able to know how long it was a million seconds ago.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:39.000
But I can't, I have no idea

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.480
It could be a week ago, it could be a month ago, it could be a year ago.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.380
In fact, let's do a scientific expirement right now.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.780
Everybody write in the comment box, no math allowed, no google searches

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.420
how long ago you think it was a million seconds ago,

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.080
like how many days, or months or years you think it was.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.020
Now I'm going to look it up, and I'm going to tell you the answer.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.180
It was, the Monday before last a million seconds ago.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.960
Interestingly, a billion, also a big number.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.760
A billion seconds ago it was not, like, last month, it was not last year, it was 1982.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:13.840
So by going from million to billion, we went from the  Monday before last

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:18.700
to, like, Michael Jackson's Thriller, and E.T., and the Falklands War.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.120
A billion to a trillion seconds ago, we're going from 1982 to 31 thousand years ago

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.280
when humans were first being introduced to Europe.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.340
"Hello, it is nice to meet you, Europe."

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.480
I don't know who was doing the introductions, also probably they didn't speak English.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.620
And it goes the other way too, like, small, tiny, super super tiny small amounts of time

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.520
like a nanosecond, you've heard of a nanosecond

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.900
but how? It's the amount of time it takes for light to travel a foot.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.020
Remember a billion seconds ago was 1982?

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.740
A billion nanoseconds ago was, by definition, one second ago.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.260
And yet, check this out,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.640
the fastest computer in the world can do 17 million calculations

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.460
in one nanosecond.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.220
I'M NOT GOING TO GET MY MIND AROUND THAT

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.040
It's amazing to me, because nobody really internalizes that information

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.840
but it allows humanity to control the world and to do so many amazing and cool things

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.440
For example:  all that math allowed us to put the Kepler space telescope in orbit

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:15.360
where it told us that there are between 15 and 30 billion Earth-like planets in our one galaxy

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.000
and there are more than 100 billion galaxies in the observable universe.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.740
and the observable universe is who knows how big of a fraction of the actual universe

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.740
which may , in fact, be infinite.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.300
NO EDGE!

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.360
Giving us yet more incomprehensible data about how amazing our universe is.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.780
We're not designed to understand that stuff, but we are designed to want to understand it.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:40.040
Which, I may be wrong, that that is the most wonderful quality of humans.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.900
This is going back to a previous conversation if you're confused.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.140
It may, in fact, be our capacity and tendency to collaborate with each other

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.560
to make things bigger than ourselves.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.920
There have also been people in the comments suggesting that empathy

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.780
is the most wonderful human quality

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.240
and that may also be true.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.560
I just kinda like the fact that there are just so many different, underappreciated reasons

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.500
that humans are, in the end, pretty cool.

